Software diversification has long been regarded as a fundamental defense principle in computer security, preventing attacks from generalizing across targets. However, this principle has been largely unexplored in modern machine learning systems. We propose neural network diversification as a defense paradigm against attacks, particularly audio adversarial examples (AEs), which we use as a representative case; more broadly, this paradigm targets attacks that exploit identical deployments by reusing crafted inputs across systems. Existing AE defenses often incur significant runtime overhead and typically rely on a uniform defense. As a result, once an attacker crafts an AE that bypasses it, the same AE can be reused to attack any victim relying on that defense. Our key insight is that, given the large number of weights in a neural network, some weights are unimportant for benign inputs yet critical for AEs. By selectively modifying such weights, we generate variants that preserve benign accuracy while improving robustness. The main challenge lies in constructing numerous orthogonal variants, such that an AE that succeeds against one variant is unlikely to succeed against another. We design effective algorithms to address this challenge. Extensive evaluation shows that our approach incurs zero runtime overhead, introduces minimal benign accuracy degradation, and remains robust against adaptive attacks.